-- gate: reject
-- defect: a TYPE name bound by TWO `exposing (..)` imports
-- rust: reject [E1012] ambiguous type name, reported at the use site
--
-- The type-namespace half of `ambiguous_unqualified_name.sky`. `Std.Ui` and
-- `Std.Css` each declare their OWN `Color` (`Std/Ui.sky:179`, `Std/Css.sky:66`)
-- — two different unions, with different constructors and different Go types.
-- Both are imported `exposing (..)` and the annotation below is bare, so which
-- union `Color` denotes was decided by which import line came LAST:
-- `hir::resolve`'s `types` map is a plain `IndexMap::insert`, and `lower`'s
-- `TypeEnv::nominal` map is documented "last-writer" on the bare name too
-- (`lower/src/lower.rs:1346-1353`). Swapping the two imports selected a
-- different Go type for the same annotation, with no diagnostic either way.
--
-- WHY THIS WAS EXCLUDED WHEN `[E1012]` LANDED FOR VALUES. Several type paths
-- SYNTHESISED a `DefId` when the target module did not really export the name —
-- kernel-implicit `Decoder` / `Value` / `Error` (which have no `type`
-- declaration in any `.sky` source) and re-exports (which `exports.rs` does not
-- chase, by design: it never recurses into another module). So one conceptual
-- type could carry two `DefId`s, and keying ambiguity on `DefId` identity would
-- have REJECTED WORKING PROGRAMS — the #164 failure mode, where a resolution fix
-- passed every corpus gate, broke a real app, and was reverted.
--
-- FIX, in the order it had to happen:
--   1. a kernel-implicit type interns into the ONE `BUILTIN_MOD` sentinel, so
--      `Decoder` is a single identity in every module that imports it, instead
--      of a fresh `DefId` per importing module;
--   2. a re-exported type is chased through the re-exporter's own import list
--      (parse only — no `resolve` recursion, so no query cycle) to the module
--      that really declares it;
--   3. anything still unresolvable — a Go FFI type, an unchaseable re-export —
--      is marked `TypeKey::Opaque` and can never join an ambiguity verdict.
--      That is a deliberate false NEGATIVE, chosen over a false REJECTION.
-- Only then does the `BindLayer` lattice (ambient < open < explicit < local)
-- run over type bindings, with the error raised at the USE SITE.
--
-- ACCEPTED TWINS (so this cannot be satisfied by a compiler that rejects
-- everything): `twin_unambiguous_type_name_is_accepted`,
-- `twin_ambiguous_type_never_referenced_is_accepted`,
-- `twin_qualified_type_reference_is_accepted`,
-- `twin_kernel_implicit_type_from_two_modules_is_accepted`,
-- `twin_type_reexported_and_imported_directly_is_accepted`,
-- `twin_builtin_type_is_ambient_and_never_ambiguous`,
-- `twin_explicit_type_exposing_beats_exposing_all` — all in `hir/src/lib.rs`.
-- Dropping either `exposing (..)` line below makes this exact file compile.
module Main exposing (main)

import Sky.Core.Prelude exposing (..)
import Std.Log exposing (println)
import Std.Ui exposing (..)
import Std.Css exposing (..)

swatch : Color -> String
swatch _c =
    "x"

-- The witness is the ANNOTATION above, not this call. An earlier version passed
-- `Ui.red`, which `Std/Ui.sky` does not define — so the file carried a second,
-- unrelated defect (an unknown name) alongside the one under test. The gate
-- asserts declared-codes ⊆ observed, so it stayed green and the extra
-- diagnostic went unnoticed; a reject witness that is red for two reasons
-- cannot show which one it is pinning.
main =
    println (swatch (Ui.rgb 255 0 0))
